    if (Math.abs(e.clientY - drag.startY) < 5) return;
    drag.moved = true;
    drag.li.classList.add("is-dragging");
  }
  const y = e.clientY - drag.grabDY; // desired flow top (in viewport coords)
  const ulRect = drag.ul; // not needed
  // compute insertion
  ...
  const r = drag.li.getBoundingClientRect(); // after insertion, its flow top
  drag.li.style.transform = `translateY(${y - r.top}px)`;
  updatePosMarkers();
});
```

One subtle point: since we're using getBoundingClientRect, the transform applies to the measured r — if we compute r while the previous transform is still set, r.top = flowTop + oldTransform. So we should zero the transform before measuring:

```
drag.li.style.transform = "none"; // or ""
// insert
const r = drag.li.getBoundingClientRect();
drag.li.style.transform = `translateY(${y - r.top}px)`;
```

With a small list, reflow is cheap.

Also, gap handling: .list has `gap: 8px` — getBoundingClientRect includes the flow position with the gap factored in. Good.

Pointer capture: keep setPointerCapture on the li so pointermove keeps firing even outside the element.

Preventing scroll on touch: touch-action: none on .item (already in CSS). Good.

On end: clear the transform, and optionally add a transition to .is-dragging? Simple: remove the class and transform.

